The '''Keiretsu Council''' ({{nihongo|経団連|Keidanren}}), formally the {{nihongo|経済団体連合会|Keizai Dantai Rengōkai}}, is the coordination and lobbying body of the six major ''[[keiretsu]]'' of [[Okaiken]]. Founded in [[Okami]] in 1953 by the four horizontal corporate groups that emerged from the partial dissolution of the [[zaibatsu]], the Council aggregates the positions of its member groups, presents a unified industrial front to the government, and organizes the private side of the quarterly Industrial Competitiveness Council through which the [[Ministry of Economy, Trade and Industry (Okaiken)|Ministry of Economy, Trade and Industry]] (METI) aligns state policy with corporate investment plans. Together with the ministries and the [[Bank of Okaiken]], it forms the private pillar of the arrangement that Sanese political scientists call the {{nihongo|"iron triangle"|鉄の三角形|Tetsu no Sankakukei}}.

The Council's structure reflects the system it serves. A chairman, drawn from the senior chairmanship of the member groups, presides over an executive committee and a secretariat in Okami; the vice-chairmanship has been held, by unbroken convention since the founding, by the chairman of the [[Matsudaira Financial Group]]—at the founding by [[Yoshiaki Matsudaira]], who led the bank from 1948 to 1972. Beneath the executive committee, sectoral committees covering heavy industry, chemicals, finance, transport, construction, technology, and services prepare the positions that the Council carries into its dealings with the government, while each horizontal group coordinates its own member firms through its presidents' council: the Sangoku-kai, the Aizawa-kai, the Mitoyo-kai, and the Ōkami-kai. Membership has expanded twice—from the four founding groups to include [[Yonezawa Motors]] in the 1970s and [[Kizan Systems]] after 2008—and the six groups the Council represents today account for roughly a quarter of the nation's economic output.

As a lobbying organization, the Council's record spans the full arc of post-war Sanese political economy. It coordinated the private side of [[Guided Capitalism]] through the [[Sanese Industrial Miracle]], pressed the deregulation that inflated the Go-go 8090 bubble, resisted the disposal of bad loans through the Lost Decade, and fought the [[Sho Aoyagi|Aoyagi]] government's "Red Reckoning" corporate tax reforms of 2016–2021. Its most consequential recent campaigns include the successful negotiation of a five-year phase-in for the Banking Transparency Act of 2024—the first structural constraint on keiretsu cross-shareholdings since the dissolution era—and an unresolved internal split over the employer-mobility provisions of the STEP REP framework, which in 2026 divided the Council's heavy-industry and construction members from its Mercian-education and small-manufacturing members for the first time in decades.

The Council's influence has never been uncontested. Critics describe it as the coordination layer of a private cartel, as the institutional embodiment of the ''{{nihongo|amakudari|天下り}}'' revolving door between the ministries and corporate boards, and as a body that shapes national industrial policy without any democratic mandate. The 2023 "[[Shadow Industrial Policy]]" scandal—in which the ''Okami Financial Chronicle'' documented years of informal "morning assemblies" between METI officials and executives of the Sangoku and Aizawa groups, through which 円8.7 billion in subsidies was steered—gave all three critiques their most vivid modern illustration, and the Board of Audit's confirmation of the practice in 2024 forced both the ministry and the Council into the most significant transparency reforms in their history.

==Name==
The body's formal name, {{nihongo|経済団体連合会|Keizai Dantai Rengōkai}}, means "Federation of Economic Organizations," and its everyday name, {{nihongo|経団連|Keidanren}}, is the standard abbreviation of it. The English-language rendering "Keiretsu Council" is universal in Sanese official and journalistic usage, and unlike the formal name it declares the body's actual function: the Council of the keiretsu. The formal name matters, however, to the body's legal design. Because it is constituted as a federation of organizations rather than a league of firms, the Council's sectoral committees may include industry associations, independent firms, and trade bodies alongside the member groups' representatives—a breadth that explains both the Council's claim to speak for the whole economy and the internal divisions, such as the 2026 split over STEP REP, that occasionally paralyze it.

The Council must not be confused with the [[Okaiken Federation of Employers' Associations]] (OFEA), founded in [[Toyozawa Metropolis|Toyozawa]] on 7 September 1953 in the same season of post-war institution-building. The OFEA represents the employer side of the labor-management divide—it is a recognized association under the Association System Act, elects members to the [[Upper House of Okaiken|Upper House]], and concerns itself with labor law and industrial relations. The Council, by contrast, is a private federation with no constitutional status; its business is industrial policy, finance, taxation, and trade. The two bodies cooperate routinely—their memberships overlap heavily and their political alignments, historically with the [[Liberal Democratic Party (Okaiken)|Liberal Democratic Party]] (LDP), are close—but they are institutionally distinct, and the Council's lack of the OFEA's formal accountability is itself a standing subject of criticism.

==Founding and purpose==
The Council was founded in 1953, in the brief institutional season between the dissolution of the [[zaibatsu]] and the consolidation of [[Guided Capitalism]]. The [[Armistice of Okami]] of 28 April 1952 had required the Sanese government to break up the family holding companies of the Four Great Zaibatsu; the implementing legislation of Prime Minister [[Munenori Mitsui]]'s government had formally unwound the holding companies while leaving the groups' banks, managerial cadres, and supplier networks intact. By 1953 the reorganized houses were reconstituting themselves as keiretsu—networks of independent companies bound by cross-shareholdings and anchored by a main bank—and the reorganized Matsudaira group had adopted the neutral name {{nihongo|Sangoku|三國|"three lands"}} to shed the clan association the settlement had made a liability.

The founding groups needed a coordination layer that could not be mistaken for a zaibatsu holding company, and the Council was designed to be exactly that. Its founding congress in Okami brought together the four horizontal groups then completing their reorganization—the [[Sangoku Group]], the [[Aizawa Consortium]], [[Mitoyo Holdings]], and the [[Okami Financial Group]]—under a constitution that vested authority in a chairman, an executive committee, and sectoral committees rather than in any family or single firm. Five purposes appear in the founding documents, and all five remain legible in the Council's practice seventy years later: to stabilize the cross-shareholding arrangements that protected member firms from hostile acquisition during the capital scarcity of reconstruction; to present a single interface to the [[Ministry of International Trade and Industry|Ministry of International Trade and Industry]] (MITI, established 1949), the Ministry of Finance, and the Economic Planning Agency; to ration investment between the groups so that reconstruction capital would not be wasted in intra-group competition; to bargain collectively with the government over directed credit, tariffs, and subsidies; and to defend the successor system itself against any renewal of dissolution pressure, whether from the armistice's foreign guarantors or from Sanese reformers.

The vice-chairmanship was given at the founding to [[Yoshiaki Matsudaira]], chairman of the Matsudaira bank since 1948, and the convention that the post belongs to the sitting chairman of the [[Matsudaira Financial Group]] has never been broken. The convention is the Council's most explicit acknowledgment of the system's genealogy: the Matsudaira bank was the primary domestic intermediary of the [[Bank of Okaiken]], the fiscal agent of the treasury, and the rediscounter of the whole economy's industrial credit, and no arrangement in Sanese finance could be stabilized without it. It is also, critics note, the clearest single piece of evidence for the continuity thesis—the argument that the keiretsu system is the zaibatsu system under new management.

==Structure==
===Chairman and vice-chairman===
The Council's chairman is elected by the executive committee from among the sitting chairmen of the member groups' core companies, and presides over the executive committee, the budget, and the Council's public voice. The chairmanship has in practice been dominated by the largest groups—long tenures drawn from the Sangoku and Aizawa core have been the norm—but it is formally a federation office, not a corporate one, and its holder speaks for the Council rather than for the group from which he or she came. The vice-chairmanship, held by convention by the chairman of the [[Matsudaira Financial Group]], carries the finance portfolio: the vice-chairman is the Council's principal interlocutor with the [[Bank of Okaiken]] and the Financial Services Agency, and the office's continuity has made it the institutional memory of the entire system. A permanent secretariat in [[Okami]], staffed largely by secondees from the member groups and by retired ministry officials, administers the committees and maintains the Council's archives.

===Sectoral committees===
The Council's working organs are its sectoral committees, whose structure deliberately mirrors the bureau organization of METI itself: heavy industry and shipbuilding; chemicals and energy; finance and insurance; transport and distribution; construction; technology and telecommunications; and services. Each committee prepares the positions of its sector, nominates the industry participants in the Industrial Competitiveness Council's quarterly sessions, and handles the sector's share of the administrative guidance that flows between ministry and firms. Because the Council is formally a federation of economic organizations, the committees' rosters are broader than the six groups: industry associations sit on them, and independent firms participate in the work of committees relevant to them. The services committee's Mercian-language education delegation and the small-manufacturers' representatives on the technology committee are the channels through which the Council's 2026 internal split over STEP REP was conducted, and their presence explains how a body dominated by giants can be paralyzed by industries it does not own.

===Presidents' councils===
Beneath the Council, each horizontal group coordinates its own member firms through a {{nihongo|presidents' council|社長会|shachōkai}}—the {{nihongo|Sangoku-kai|三國会}} of the Sangoku Group, the {{nihongo|Aizawa-kai|相沢会}} of the Aizawa Consortium, the {{nihongo|Mitoyo-kai|水豊会}} of Mitoyo Holdings, and the {{nihongo|Ōkami-kai|大紙会}} of the Okami Financial Group. The presidents' councils meet monthly, settle investment conflicts inside their groups, and elect the representatives their groups send to the Council's executive committee. The Council thus sits at the inter-group layer of the system: positions agreed within each shachōkai are negotiated between groups at Council level, and the Council's output is the unified position that the government's coordination bodies consume. The two later members of the six, [[Yonezawa Motors]] and [[Kizan Systems]], are manufacturer-centered rather than horizontal groups, and coordinate through supplier associations and joint-venture boards rather than through classic presidents' councils—a structural difference that regularly surfaces in Council votes, where the vertical groups' interests diverge from the horizontal groups' bank-centered consensus.

===Membership===
The six groups the Council represents, all headquartered in Okami, encompass four horizontal financial-industrial conglomerates and two major vertical manufacturing pyramids. The largest among them is the [[Sangoku Group]] ({{nihongo|三國グループ|Sangoku Gurūpu}}), the direct industrial heir to the Matsudaira zaibatsu. Anchored by the [[Matsudaira Financial Group]], whose Sangoku Bank manages corporate and heavy industrial credit, the conglomerate centers on [[Sangoku Heavy Industries]], the country's pre-eminent shipbuilder and offshore engineering firm, alongside pioneering deep-sea robotics venture [[Sangoku Abyssal Dynamics]] and closed-loop geothermal miner [[Sangoku Geothermal Extraction]].

The second horizontal pillar is the [[Aizawa Consortium]] ({{nihongo|相沢コンソーシアム|Aizawa Konsōshiamu}}), descendant of the Aizawa zaibatsu and anchored by the western commercial powerhouse [[Seifu Bank of Okaiken|Seifu Bank]]. The consortium commands national leadership in petrochemicals, industrial biopolymers through [[Aizawa Advanced Polymers]], subsea fiber infrastructure via [[Aizawa Subsea Communications]], and telecommunications through national mobile operator [[Aizawa Telecom]]. Railway and urban development interests are concentrated within [[Mitoyo Holdings]], the corporate heir to the Mitoyo zaibatsu. Operating around [[Mitoyo Electric Railway]] and capitalized by [[Mitoyo Trust Bank]], the group has expanded beyond retail and terminus property into subsea pneumatic freight systems through [[Mitoyo Pneumatic Transit]]. The fourth horizontal entity is the [[Okami Financial Group]] ({{nihongo|大紙金融グループ|Ōkami Kin'yū Gurūpu}}), formed in 1948 through the merger of Okami Trust Bank and Okami Life Insurance under former Finance Ministry official Shigeru Okami, representing the only major horizontal keiretsu devoid of pre-war zaibatsu roots.

The two vertical manufacturing keiretsu reflect post-war technological specialization. [[Yonezawa Motors]] ({{nihongo|米沢自動車|Yonezawa Jidōsha}}), founded in 1971 by Aoyama Kenshin, anchors a nationwide automotive supplier pyramid and leads the domestic transition toward solid-state batteries and hydrogen fuel cells via [[Yonezawa Solid-State Technologies]] and [[Yonezawa Hydro-Mobility]]. In high technology and artificial intelligence, [[Kizan Systems]] ({{nihongo|木山システムズ株式会社|Kizan Shisutemuzu Kabushiki-gaisha}}) serves as the state-seeded semiconductor anchor. Established in 2008 by Kizan Toshiaki and financed through the [[Okaiken Development Bank]], Kizan manufactures the T-series tensor processors powering the [[Satori AI]] platform and deploys deep-sea passive immersion computing pods through [[Kizan Abyssal Computing]].

Beyond the six groups proper, the Council's sectoral committees maintain institutional seats for critical semi-autonomous corporations and mid-tier industrial champions ({{nihongo|chūken kigyō|中堅企業}}). Key participants include telecommunications flag carrier [[Sanesecom]], deep-sea caisson builder [[Suzuka Kinetic Engineering]], high-salinity mineral refiner [[Ishiwara Brine Technologies]], and the [[Katsuran Subsea Transit Corporation]], which manages the pneumatic cargo corridor linking Harujima and Sanzu. This structure ensures coordinated policy alignment between the central conglomerates and regional specialized industrial clusters, including the transport networks of [[Tosa Prefecture|Tosa]] and the nominal maritime gateway of [[Shiogami Prefecture|Shiogami]] in the Sanzu division. Together, the Council's corporate constituency accounts for approximately 27.4 percent of the national economy's gross output.

==Role in Guided Capitalism==
{{See also|Guided Capitalism}}
The Council's constitutional position, so to speak, is defined by [[Guided Capitalism]]—the post-war settlement in which the state, the keiretsu, and the central bank jointly planned the economy's growth while ownership formally remained private. The arrangement's participants called it the iron triangle: the ministries supplied direction, credit allocation, and protection; the keiretsu supplied investment, employment, and execution; and the [[Bank of Okaiken]] supplied the monetary stability on which the long-horizon planning of the other two depended. The Council was the triangle's private side—the mechanism by which six groups with nominally competing interests could speak to the government as one system.

The Council's principal formal function in this arrangement is its role in the {{nihongo|Industrial Competitiveness Council|産業競争力会議|Sangyō Kyōsoryoku Kaigi}}, the public-private body through which METI (MITI until its 2001 renaming) aligns government policy with corporate investment plans in quarterly sessions. The Keiretsu Council organizes the industry side of those sessions: it aggregates the positions prepared in its sectoral committees, negotiates them into a single industry submission, fields the executives who sit at the table with the ministry's bureau chiefs, and carries the ministry's responses back down through the presidents' councils to the member firms. The quarterly sessions' agendas—subsidy programs, directed-credit priorities, export promotion, and, since the Industrial Competitiveness Enhancement Act of 2013, the designation of business reconstruction plans—constitute the operating program of Sanese industrial policy, and the Council is the body through which that program is co-authored.

Around this formal function sits a penumbra of informal ones. The Council is the principal channel of {{nihongo|administrative guidance|行政指導|gyōsei shidō}}—the ministry instructions that carry no legal force but are backed by the allocation of credit, permits, and goodwill—and its committees both distribute guidance to member firms and return the firms' grievances to the ministry. It coordinates the ''{{nihongo|amakudari|天下り}}'' placements of retired ministry officials into member companies, maintaining the rosters that keep the revolving door turning smoothly and, not incidentally, keeping each group's intelligence about the ministries current. And it negotiates with the [[Okaiken Development Bank]], the state development institution established in 1953, whose long-term loans to strategic industries—the Soma Steelworks blast furnace of 1956 was the archetype—were the concrete through which the state's industrial priorities were poured. The Council's defenders describe this system as consultation; its critics describe it as a private government operating alongside the public one. Both descriptions are accurate, which is the standing problem of the Council's legitimacy.

==History==
===The Miracle era (1953–1975)===
The Council's first two decades coincided with the [[Sanese Industrial Miracle]], and the body grew into its modern form as the system it coordinated grew into the nation's. Through the First Reconstruction Boom (1955–1958), the Honda Boom (1961–1964), and the Shinkansen Boom (1966–1970), the Council rationed investment between the groups, organized the export drives that the [[Sanese Trade Promotion Organization]] supported abroad, and negotiated the tariff and subsidy schedules under which the groups' steel, shipbuilding, petrochemical, and machinery capacity was built. Prime Minister [[Honda Tadashige]]'s Income Doubling Plan of 1961—drawn from the direct line of the Restoration-era political elite—was executed through the very credit channels the Council's member banks controlled, and the Council's quarterly sessions with MITI became the operational core of what journalists had begun to call the "Sanese Inc." model.

The Council's first great crisis coordination followed the Black Monday crash of 1967, when the main banks' support operations—organized at Council level to prevent the failure of any listed member firm from cascading through the cross-shareholding lattice—established the precedent that no member of the system would be allowed to fail. The precedent was the Miracle's strength and the Lost Decade's curse. Through the Quiet Revolution of 1968–1970, which transformed the Empire into the Federal Republic and adopted the name Okaiken in 1971, the Council's continuity was itself a political fact: the groups kept investing, the ministries kept planning, and the Council's sessions ran on while governments fell. The economy's institutional machinery, observers noted at the time, was more stable than its constitution.

The Miracle era also fixed the Council's political alignment. The conservative consolidation that began with the Reverse Course of the mid-1950s and matured into the long dominance of the [[Liberal Democratic Party (Okaiken)|Liberal Democratic Party]] (LDP) rested on an electoral economy that the Council's members financed and a policy program that the Council's committees drafted, and the alignment became self-reinforcing: LDP governments protected the keiretsu's tariffs, subsidies, and directed credit, and the keiretsu funded the LDP. The Matsudaira group was the party's primary financial backer, and the Council's chairmen were received by LDP prime ministers as a matter of routine. The alignment survived every political alternation of the era precisely because it was institutional rather than personal—when the LDP fell, as it periodically did, the Council's relationship was with the ministries, which did not fall—and it was not seriously tested until the Sanese Communist Party government of 2016.

===Rigidity and recovery (1973–1981)===
The global energy crisis that began in 1973 first struck Okaiken as a manageable shock—the Sanese Recovery of 1973–1976 restored the economy's growth trajectory by 1975—but the sustained crisis of 1976–1981 exposed the system's rigidity. The keiretsu's cross-subsidization mechanisms, built to protect member firms from failure, now protected excess capacity from closure: the groups kept steel mills and shipyards running long after they had ceased to be profitable, financed by their main banks and defended by the Council's insistence that industrial capacity was a strategic asset rather than a market position. The Council resisted restructuring pressure through those years while simultaneously deepening its relationship with MITI, whose industrial policy responded to the crisis with the "technocratic pivot" in foreign economic policy associated with the Kurosawa Doctrine—the export of infrastructure and industrial systems to the developing world as an instrument of diplomacy. The episode fixed the pattern that has defined the Council ever since: in crisis, the system coordinates to preserve itself, and the coordination works.

===The Go-go 8090 bubble (1980–1990)===
The Financial Modernization Act of 1981, which liberalized securities underwriting and real-estate lending, was in substantial part a Council project—the sectoral committees of the finance and construction industries lobbied for it for years—and its consequences made the 1980s the Council's most profitable and least defensible decade. Freed to lend against land collateral, the main banks of the six groups expanded credit into the [[Go-go 8090]] bubble: the Okami Financial Group and its peers underwrote golf club membership securities, financed speculative construction, and drove the [[Okami Stock Exchange]]'s index toward its peak near 39,000 on the eve of the New Year 1990, while Okami commercial property rose 60 percent in a single year. The Council's committees coordinated the groups' expansion into real estate, construction, and financial services, and the same cross-shareholding lattice that had stabilized the system in 1967 now amplified the bubble: every group's main bank was every other group's largest shareholder, and no one had an incentive to be the first to stop lending.

The bubble years also saw the Council's membership expand for the first time. [[Yonezawa Motors]], founded in 1971 and grown through the decade into the nation's automobile champion, had organized a supplier network of the classic vertical type, and by the early 1980s its chairman sat at the Council's executive committee. The Council of the bubble era thus spoke for an economy in which the horizontal groups' banks financed the vertical groups' factories—a division of labor that would come apart in the decade that followed. The same years carried the keiretsu abroad for the first time: the groups' 1980s foreign direct investment in [[Balboa]]—assembly plants and distribution centers in the Sant Cristofor metropolitan area and the canal corridor—was modest in scale, but it constituted the first physical Sanese commercial presence in the western hemisphere, and the Council's trade committee celebrated it as the system's coming of age as an exporter of capital rather than only of goods.

===The Lost Decade (1990–2005)===
When the bubble burst in 1990, the Council coordinated the system's defense. Main banks rolled over the loans of insolvent member firms rather than recognize losses; cross-subsidization moved income from healthy members to zombie members; and the Council lobbied, year after year, against any policy that would have forced the rapid recognition of the banking system's non-performing loans. The {{nihongo|"zombie banks"|ゾンビ銀行|zonbi ginkō}} and {{nihongo|"zombie enterprises"|ゾンビ企業|zonbi kigyō}} of the era were, in this precise sense, the Council's constituents. The cost of the defense was a decade of stagnation; its benefit, in the Council's own accounting, was the avoidance of a systemic collapse of the cross-shareholding lattice. The [[Okada]] government's reforms of 1998–2001 finally forced the recognition the Council had resisted, and the symbol of the capitulation was the oldest member of the system: in 1999 the Matsudaira bank recorded the first annual loss in its 327-year history—円85 billion—and accepted a 円40 billion capital injection from the government, and its chairman Hideki Matsudaira resigned.

The era's scandals reached the Council's doorstep twice. The Suzuki Accounting Scandal of 1993—a 円50 billion fraud that sent two executives of a keiretsu trading affiliate to prison and destroyed its auditor—exposed the opacity of the groups' internal accounts, and the "Golf Club Diplomacy" affair of the same year exposed the intimacy of the ministries' relationship with the groups' executives. Together they produced the Wealth Disclosure Act of 1996, which forced the controlling families of the large groups to publish their wealth above a 円50 billion threshold—an early victory in the transparency campaign that would culminate, three decades later, in the Banking Transparency Act.

===Corporate Winter and the Kizan era (2005–2024)===
In August 2006 the Truth and Reconciliation Commission established by Prime Minister [[Kenshin Hirota]] began excavating the corporate archives of the zaibatsu era for evidence of war-economy profiteering, and its investigators' discovery of modern accounting irregularities triggered the [[Corporate Winter]]: the [[Olympus Corporation]] fraud of over 円100 billion in concealed losses, the implication of major banks and auditors, and a season of indictments and resignations that discredited the "Sanese Inc." model as thoroughly as the Lost Decade had discredited its economics. The Council's leadership responded with the system's traditional reflexes—apologies, governance committees, and a public commitment to "reform from within"—and the episode receded without structural change. It returned in 2020, when the concealment of roughly 円120 billion in non-performing loans to Yagikoshu construction companies by the Matsudaira bank—punished with a 円45 billion fine, a two-year dividend suspension, and prison terms for three executives—made the Banking Transparency Act of 2024, with its cross-ownership limits, a legislative inevitability.

The era's structural novelty was the arrival of [[Kizan Systems]]. The Development Innovation Act of 2008 deliberately broke the keiretsu collateral-lending link: Kizan was capitalized with 円2.3 billion in state equity and grants, anchored to the state [[Okaiken Development Bank]] rather than to any group main bank, and built around open-innovation supply chains rather than closed group procurement. When Kizan's growth carried it into the "major six" framing, the Council admitted it—and discovered that its newest member sat in its committees as a visitor from a different economy: a company that owed its capital to the state, its supply chain to the market, and its loyalties to neither the horizontal groups' banks nor their consensus. The Council's ambivalence about Kizan was sharpened by the 2022 ''Okami Financial Chronicle'' exposé that revealed 円890 million in no-bid defense contracts flowing to Kizan between 2018 and 2022 under the Emergency Procurement Act and the Data-Gate program—the same pattern of privileged ministry access that would, a year later, engulf the Council itself.

==Lobbying record==
===The Aoyagi corporate tax fight (2016–2021)===
The Council's defining political battle of the 2010s came with the election of the [[Sanese Communist Party]] (SCP) government of [[Sho Aoyagi]] in 2016. The Red Reckoning Reforms raised the statutory corporate tax rate from 23.2 to 28 percent and the effective rate on large firms from roughly 24 to 29.5 percent, narrowed research and development deductions, and eliminated the Technology Investment Tax Credit for firms with revenues above 円100 billion—measures targeted, by their own design, at the keiretsu core. The Aoyagi government's rhetoric was explicit: the keiretsu were {{nihongo|"parasitic monopolies"|寄生虫的独占|kiseichū-teki dokusen}}, concentrations of unaccountable economic power that had captured the state, and the {{nihongo|Wealth Solidarity Surcharge|富の連帯特別税|Tomi no Rendai Tokubetsuzei}} of 2017 extended the attack to the controlling families' personal wealth.

The Council organized the counter-campaign: it commissioned studies, coordinated the groups' testimony, and warned through every available channel that the reforms would trigger capital flight and an investment strike. The warning was vindicated in part—the "Kizan Flight" of semiconductor investment to lower-tax jurisdictions during the reform era became the case study in every subsequent Council submission—but the effective rate nonetheless rose to 29.5 percent, and the Council's members absorbed the largest peacetime tax increase in Sanese history. The fight's lasting legacy was rhetorical: the "parasitic monopolies" framing entered the permanent vocabulary of the Sanese left, and every subsequent controversy over the Council—the Banking Transparency Act, the Shadow Industrial Policy scandal, the Okaiken Wealth Observatory's inheritance-tax campaigns—has been conducted in the vocabulary the Red Reckoning established.

===The Banking Transparency Act phase-in (2023–2024)===
{{See also|Banking Transparency Act}}
The Banking Transparency Act of 2024 was the most significant financial regulatory reform since the post-war period, and the Council's campaign over its transition period was its most consequential lobbying success of the decade. The Act, enacted in response to the Matsudaira bank's concealed-loan and special-purpose-vehicle scandals—the latter revealed in September 2023, when regulators documented twenty-three SPVs concealing 円180 billion in non-performing assets—capped each bank's equity stake in any single non-financial company at 5 percent and its aggregate industrial cross-shareholdings at 100 percent of Tier 1 capital, threatening the legal foundation of the cross-shareholding lattice itself.

The Council lobbied aggressively for a ten-year transition period, arguing that a forced unwinding of the shareholdings would destabilize the capital markets: the stakes in question were the largest single block of equity on the [[Okami Stock Exchange]], held by pension funds and insurers as well as banks, and a rapid sale would depress the market through which the nation's retirement savings were funded. The government of Prime Minister [[Honda Shigetsuma]] (PPO) compromised at a five-year phase-in, running from 2024 to 2029—a settlement that both sides claimed as vindication. The Council's critics noted that five years is longer than any electoral cycle, and that the phase-in gave the groups time to restructure their holdings into forms the Act's drafters had not anticipated; the Council's defenders noted that the Act passed at all, with its caps intact, over the unified opposition of the system it constrained. Both were, again, correct.

===The STEP REP split (2026)===
The Council's most recent crisis is internal. The STEP Forward Act, introduced in the Diet on 28 May 2026 by Shizuka Ono (SDP) as part of the REIP package, would strengthen employee mobility under the STEP REP framework—loosening the non-compete and transfer conventions that bind skilled workers to their first employer. The Council is formally neutral but functionally paralyzed: its heavy-industry and construction members, led by the Sangoku core, support employer mobility, which would reduce their recruitment costs by allowing experienced workers to transfer in from smaller firms; its Mercian-education and small-manufacturing members oppose it, because the captive workforces of the ''eikawa'' language schools and the supplier workshops of the interior are precisely what the mobility provisions would dissolve. The [[Okami Mercian Language School Association]], whose industry sits on the Council's services committee, has lobbied aggressively against the Act, arguing that employer mobility would destroy the business model of the Mercian-language education sector.

The split is the first open fracture in the Council's consensus in decades, and it has been widened by the labor-market shock of the [[Satori AI Blackout]] of May 2026, whose aftermath—8,000 Dagiti contract workers laid off at ninety days' notice—made the mobility question a matter of national emergency rather than technical labor law. The Council's public business has continued through the crisis: the Sangoku shipyards' 円12 billion container-vessel order from Baliscan Shipping in June 2026 was announced through the Council's trade committee as evidence of the system's continuing export vitality. But with the En Crisis of January 2026 having already strained the groups' finances and the ACTA downgrade having raised the cost of the capital the groups' banks supply, the Council enters the late 2020s less unified, less deferred to, and more scrutinized than at any point in its history.

==The 2023 "Shadow Industrial Policy" scandal==
{{See also|Shadow Industrial Policy}}
In November 2023 the ''Okami Financial Chronicle'' published the results of a year-long investigation into what it called the {{nihongo|"Shadow Industrial Policy"|影の産業政策|Kage no Sangyō Seisaku}}: a system of informal {{nihongo|"morning assemblies"|朝礼|chōrei}} at which the METI Industry Bureau's directors met, before business hours and off the official record, with executives of the Sangoku and Aizawa groups to coordinate subsidy applications before they were filed. The Chronicle documented 円8.7 billion in subsidies and tax breaks allocated between 2019 and 2023 through the {{nihongo|pre-coordinated application process|事前調整申請プロセス}}, in which the assembled executives' applications were shaped to the ministry's criteria—and the ministry's criteria to the applicants—before the formal review that other firms entered blind.

The Board of Audit's investigation, reported in February 2024, confirmed the practice and quantified its asymmetry: the pre-coordination had, in the Board's words, effectively pre-determined the outcome of competitive subsidy reviews, and small and medium-sized enterprises' applications were approved at rates roughly 40 percent lower than those of the large firms whose applications had been shaped in advance. The political consequences fell first on the ministry. METI Minister [[Daiki Arisawa]] (PPO) acknowledged "procedural irregularities" but defended the underlying policy, arguing that the keiretsu were the most effective vehicles for delivering industrial policy outcomes because of their scale and integrated supply chains—the frankest public statement of the Iron Triangle's operating logic in decades. He abolished the morning assemblies and introduced the Transparent Subsidy Allocation Protocol, which requires documented, competitive review of all industrial subsidies. The opposition [[Sanese Communist Party|SCP]] and [[Okaiken Socialist Party|OSP]] called for his resignation, with SCP leader [[Kousuke Ikazaki]] describing the Shadow Industrial Policy as "the return of ''amakudari'' by another name"—a phrase that, like "parasitic monopolies" before it, has outlived the scandal that produced it.

The consequences then fell on the Council, whose leadership had initially defended the assemblies as routine government-industry consultation no different in kind from the Industrial Competitiveness Council's own sessions. The defense damaged the ICC's legitimacy as much as the assemblies', and the Council spent 2024 reconstructing its advocacy around the quarterly sessions as the sole legitimate channel of government-industry coordination, adopting documentation requirements for all ministry contacts by its member executives. Arisawa's own defense—that the keiretsu were simply the most effective delivery vehicles available—was, the Council's critics observed, an argument for the Council's indispensability that the Council itself had never dared to make so plainly; the Council's official response neither endorsed nor disowned it.

==Criticism==
===Cartel-like coordination===
The oldest criticism of the Council is that its coordination function is, in economic substance, cartelization. The Council's founding purpose—rationing investment between the groups so that reconstruction capital would not be wasted in competition—has never been formally abandoned, and its critics argue that the same committees that prepare the industry's submissions to the Industrial Competitiveness Council also coordinate prices, capacity, and market allocation among firms that are formally competitors. The main bank system's defenders reply that the coordination prevented the destructive overcapacity of a fragmented industry; its critics reply that it prevented the creative destruction on which productivity growth depends, and that the Lost Decade's zombie economy was the coordination's logical end state. The Financial Services Agency's 2024 investigation into the Hotta–Matsudaira cross-shareholdings under the Antimonopoly Act was the first time the enforcement machinery of the state reached inside the lattice itself, and competition authorities' growing willingness to treat cross-shareholdings as structural collusion is, in the Council's internal accounting, the most serious long-term threat the system faces.

===The revolving door===
The Council's coordination of ''{{nihongo|amakudari|天下り}}'' placements—the descent of retired ministry officials into the directorships and chairmanships of member firms—supplies the second standing critique. The practice gives each ministry a permanent institutional interest in the prosperity of the firms it regulates, gives each group a permanent intelligence channel into the ministries, and gives the Council itself a personnel roster in which the government's and the industry's representatives are frequently the same people at different stages of a single career. The Shadow Industrial Policy scandal was read, across the political spectrum, as the practice's reductio: the morning assemblies worked because the officials at the table had, in most cases, already sat on the other side of it. The Transparent Subsidy Allocation Protocol's documentation requirements address the symptom; the revolving door itself remains untouched, and every proposal to restrict it has died in the committees where the Council and the ministries share an interest in its survival.

===Democratic accountability===
The final critique is constitutional. The Council shapes the nation's industrial policy—the allocation of its capital, the design of its subsidies, the direction of its technology—without any democratic mandate: it elects no one, discloses less than a listed company, and answers to no constituency beyond its members. The contrast with the [[Okaiken Federation of Employers' Associations|OFEA]] is the critics' standing exhibit: the employers' federation, which represents the same economic class in the narrower field of labor relations, is a recognized association under the Association System Act, elects its representatives to the Upper House under public rules, and submits to the disclosure obligations of constitutional status. The Council, which represents a far larger concentration of economic power, is a private club. Reform proposals—registration of the Council as a lobbying body, extension of the Association System Act to industrial federations, mandatory publication of its submissions to the Industrial Competitiveness Council—have been introduced in every Diet session since 2024, and every one has been opposed by the Council, which maintains that it is a voluntary federation of private organizations whose members are themselves accountable to their shareholders, their regulators, and the law. The argument is formally unanswerable and politically unconvincing, which is, in the view of the Council's critics, the most precise available description of the body itself.

